position & ANSI Sequences - **Status:** Accepted - **Date:** 2026-11-08 - **Deciders:** Rich Architectural Maintainers ## Context Styling in...\n2. [KI] **ADR-0003: Style Composition & ANSI Sequences** (`.ki-base/decisions/0003-style-composition-and-ansi.md`, score: 6.361)\n   > # ADR-0003: Style Composition & ANSI Sequences - **Status:** Accepted - **Date:** 2026-11-08 - **Deciders:** Rich Architectural Maintainers ## Context Styling in...\n3. [KI] **ADR-0002: Render Protocol & Component Isolation** (`.ki-base/decisions/0002-render-protocol.md`, score: 5.329)\n   > # ADR-0002: Render Protocol & Component Isolation - **Status:** Accepted - **Date:** 2026-11-08 - **Deciders:** Rich Architectural Maintainers ## Context Rich supports...\n4. [KI] **ADR-0002: Render Protocol & Component Isolation** (`.ki-base/decisions/0002-render-protocol.md`, score: 5.329)\n   > # ADR-0002: Render Protocol & Component Isolation - **Status:** Accepted - **Date:** 2026-11-08 - **Deciders:** Rich Architectural Maintainers ## Context Rich supports...\n5. [KI] **KI: Rich Core (Internal Utilities & Platform Adaptors)** (`.ki-base/knowledge/KI_rich_null.md`, score: 4.884)\n   > ...Overview Provides internal mathematical routines, layout ratio solvers, text wrapping primitives, platform Win32 console structures, and base ABC protocols. It forms the low-level foundation for rendering...\n\n*Showing 5 of 18 results. Call `ki_search(query='render protocol measurement segments Style composition ratio', page=2)` (or `offset=5`) to view more.*"}],"details":{"server":"ki-manager","tool":"ki_search"},"isError":false,"durationMs":82,"timestamp":1794168271007},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"toolu_01JPp4x17KqoGKJZVZHZ9Rmq","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"0001-terminal-segments.md\n0002-render-protocol.md\n0003-style-composition-and-ansi.md\n0004-ratio-distribution-and-layout.md\n# ADR-0002: Render Protocol & Component Isolation\n\n- **Status:** Accepted\n- **Date:** 2026-11-08\n- **Deciders:** Rich Architectural Maintainers\n\n## Context\n\nRich supports arbitrary nested UI components (such as Panels inside Tables inside Trees). If custom components were allowed to mutate shared rendering options or directly manipulate the `Console` buffer, rendering would become non-deterministic, thread-unsafe, and incompatible with real-time displays like `Live` and `Progress`.\n\n## Decision\n\n1. **Mandatory Console Protocol:**  \n   All visual components must implement the public generator protocol:\n   ```python\n   def __rich_console__(self, console: \"Console\", options: \"ConsoleOptions\") -> \"RenderResult\":\n       ...\n       yield Segment(...)\n   ```\n   A component may optionally implement `__rich_measure__(self, console: \"Console\", options: \"ConsoleOptions\") -> Measurement` for dynamic sizing.\n\n2. **Strict Component Isolation Invariants:**\n   - **No Buffer Access:** Components must NEVER access or write to `console._buffer` or `console.file` directly. All output must be returned via `yield` as an iterable of `Segment` or renderable objects.\n   - **Options Immutability:** The passed `options: ConsoleOptions` instance is strictly read-only. Components must never mutate its fields. To adjust constraints for child components, use `options.update(...)` which returns a fresh copy.\n   - **Stateless Yielding:** Generators must be re-entrant and idempotent so that multiple render passes (e.g. measuring then rendering) produce identical results without side effects.\n\n3. **Internal Spacing Convention for Inline Badges:**\n   - In accordance with repository design conventions, inline badge components must enclose their inner text with exactly one space of padding inside the boundary caps: `\"( \"` + content + `\" )\"` for pill badges, and `\"[\" + \" \"` + content + `\" ]\"` for square badges.\n   - Example: `Badge(\"DEPLOYED\", pill=True, icon=\"★\")` must yield segments rendering as `\"( ★ DEPLOYED )\"`. Square badges render as `\"[ SYSTEM ALERT ]\"`. Measurement must include the 4 cells occupied by borders and inner spaces.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- Components can be composed arbitrarily without side effects.\n- Direct console buffer writes or mutating shared options is treated as an architectural violation and rejected in code review.\n- Ensures seamless integration with `Live` displays and virtual screen buffers.\n\"\"\"Rich text and beautiful formatting in the terminal.\"\"\"\n\nimport os\nfrom typing import IO, TYPE_CHECKING, Any, Callable, Optional, Union\n\nfrom ._extension import load_ipython_extension  # noqa: F401\n\n__all__ = [\"get_console\", \"reconfigure\", \"print\", \"inspect\", \"print_json\"]\n\nif TYPE_CHECKING:\n    from .console import Console\n\n# Global console used by alternative print\n_console: Optional[\"Console\"] = None\n\ntry:\n    _IMPORT_CWD = os.path.abspath(os.getcwd())\nexcept FileNotFoundError:\n    # Can happen if the cwd has been deleted\n    _IMPORT_CWD = \"\"\n\n\ndef get_console() -> \"Console\":\n    \"\"\"Get a global :class:`~rich.console.Console` instance. This function is used when Rich requires a Console,\n    and hasn't been explicitly given one.\n\n    Returns:\n        Console: A console instance.\n    \"\"\"\n    global _console\n    if _console is None:\n        from .console import Console\n\n        _console = Console()\n\n    return _console\n\n\ndef reconfigure(*args: Any, **kwargs: Any) -> None:\n    \"\"\"Reconfigures the global console by replacing it with another.\n\n    Args:\n        *args (Any): Positional arguments for the replacement :class:`~rich.console.Console`.\n        **kwargs (Any): Keyword arguments for the replacement :class:`~rich.console.Console`.\n    \"\"\"\n    from rich.console import Console\n\n    new_console = Console(*args, **kwargs)\n    _console = get_console()\n    _console.__dict__ = new_console.__dict__\n\n\ndef print(\n    *objects: Any,\n    sep: str = \" \",\n    end: str = \"\\n\",\n    file: Optional[IO[str]] = None,\n    flush: bool = False,\n) -> None:\n    r\"\"\"Print object(s) supplied via positional arguments.\n M .ki-base/DIR_INDEX.md\n M .ki-base/config.json\n M .ki-base/doc_config.json\n M .ki-base/knowledge/KI_rich_align.md\n M .ki-base/knowledge/KI_rich_null.md\n M .ki-base/knowledge/KI_rich_region.md\n M .ki-base/knowledge/KI_rich_syntax.md\n M AGENTS.md\n?? .ki-base/decisions/\n"}],"isError":false,"durationMs":5118,"timestamp":1794168271011},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I'm checking the ADR's pill and square formats against the task spec, and both use a space inside the brackets, so they match. The ADR also says measurement has to count the four border and inner-space cells around the content, which fits the same layout.\n\nI'm going to read ADR 0001 and 0003 next, plus the rest of the `__init__.py` exports, and look at a simple existing renderable like `rule.py` or `padding.py` to see the conventions. I also want to check how ADR 0003 handles style lookup through `Console.get_style`.\n\n","thinkingSignature":".eyJlbmRwb2ludF9zbHVnIjoiYW50aHJvcGljL2NsYXVkZS1oYWlrdS01LjUtMjAyNjEwMDd8Y2xhdWRlLW9uLWF3cyJ9"},{"type":"text","text":"The ADR-0002 spacing rule matches the spec (`( ` … ` )` and `[ ` … ` ]`, with 4 cells of border and padding). Next I'll read the remaining style and segment ADRs and check how `__init__.py` exports names."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_01HVrtp7Xy674bMNfqv8HAnK","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /app && cat .ki-base/decisions/0003-style-composition-and-ansi.md | head -70; echo ----; cat .ki-base/decisions/0001-terminal-segments.md | head -50; echo ----; grep -n \"__all__\\|^from\\|^import\" rich/__init__.py | head; echo ----; sed -n 1,40p rich/padding.py | head -5; ls tests | head -80"}}],"api":"anthropic-messages","provider":"openrouter","model":"anthropic/claude-haiku-5.5","usage":{"input":2,"output":569,"cacheRead":9448,"cacheWrite":2934,"totalTokens":12953,"cost":{"input":2.0000000000000002e-7,"output":0.0002845,"cacheRead":0.00009448,"cacheWrite":0.00036675,"total":0.00074593,"totalFromProvider":true},"cacheWrite1h":0,"reasoning":301},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1794168271037,"providerThinkingLevel":"medium","responseId":"msg_011Cgr4UMadj9MWM7jX9Z7Qv","rawStopReason":"tool_use","durationMs":3159,"thinkingLevel":"medium"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"toolu_01HVrtp7Xy674bMNfqv8HAnK","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# ADR-0003: Style Composition & ANSI Sequences\n\n- **Status:** Accepted\n- **Date:** 2026-11-08\n- **Deciders:** Rich Architectural Maintainers\n\n## Context\n\nStyling in terminals requires combining foreground colors, background colors, links, and various text attributes (bold, italic, underline, reverse, etc.). Rich needs an immutable, high-performance styling algebra that supports cascading inheritance, markup parsing, and export to both ANSI terminals and web formats (HTML/SVG).\n\n## Decision\n\n1. **Immutable Style Class:**  \n   The `Style` object is immutable and hashable with internal caching:\n   - Attributes (bold, underline, double_underline, italic, blink, etc.) use tri-state values (`True`, `False`, `None` where `None` indicates inherit/unset).\n2. **Style Composition Algebra (`+` operator):**  \n   Style addition (`style1 + style2`) cascades attributes from left to right:\n   - Attributes defined in `style2` override attributes in `style1` if set (`is not None`).\n   - Unset attributes (`None`) in `style2` inherit values from `style1`.\n3. **ANSI Sequence Mapping & Exporters:**  \n   Every new style modifier must maintain synchronization across:\n   - Terminal ANSI code generator: open code (e.g. `\\x1b[4:2m` or `\\x1b[21m`) and reset/close code.\n   - Default style definitions (`rich/default_styles.py`).\n   - Markup parser (`rich/markup.py`) to recognize `[modifier]...[/]`.\n   - Exporters (`rich/terminal_theme.py`, `rich/_export_format.py`) for SVG and HTML rendering.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- Full consistency between CLI console output, HTML export, and SVG snapshots.\n- Guarantees predictable cascading style resolution across nested tags.\n----\n# ADR-0001: Terminal Segments & Atomic Decomposition\n\n- **Status:** Accepted\n- **Date:** 2026-11-08\n- **Deciders:** Rich Architectural Maintainers\n\n## Context\n\nTerminal emulators operate on streams of characters interspersed with ANSI escape codes for styling and cursor movement. Without a unified primitive abstraction, high-level components (tables, syntax, markdown) would have to manually track escape codes, calculate visual cell widths, and handle cross-platform line breaks, leading to exponential complexity and bugs.\n\n## Decision\n\n1. **Atomic Segment Primitive:**  \n   Every visual element in Rich must ultimately reduce to a stream of `Segment` instances:\n   ```python\n   class Segment(NamedTuple):\n       text: str\n       style: Optional[Style] = None\n       control: Optional[Sequence[ControlCode]] = None\n   ```\n2. **Strict Invariants:**\n   - A `Segment` text piece must not contain unescaped newline characters `\\n` or `\\r` unless processed by `Segment.split_lines()` or `Segment.split_and_crop_lines()`.\n   - Visual measurement is derived strictly from `cell_len(segment.text)` using East Asian Width Unicode tables, never raw `len(text)`.\n   - Control sequences (cursor up, color palette queries) are encapsulated in the optional `control` field and take zero visual cell length.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- All rendering operations are stateless stream-processing pipelines.\n- Layout algorithms only need to measure and slice `Segment` collections.\n- Clean separation between logical layout calculation and physical terminal escape emission.\n----\n3:import os\n4:from typing import IO, TYPE_CHECKING, Any, Callable, Optional, Union\n6:from ._extension import load_ipython_extension  # noqa: F401\n8:__all__ = [\"get_console\", \"reconfigure\", \"print\", \"inspect\", \"print_json\"]\n----\nfrom typing import TYPE_CHECKING, List, Optional, Tuple, Union\n\nif TYPE_CHECKING:\n    from .console import (\n        Console,\n__init__.py\n__pycache__\n_card_render.py\nconftest.py\npytest.ini\nrender.py\ntest_align.py\ntest_ansi.py\ntest_bar.py\ntest_block_bar.py\ntest_box.py\ntest_card.py\ntest_cells.py\ntest_color.py\ntest_color_triplet.py\ntest_columns.py\ntest_columns_align.py\ntest_console.py\ntest_constrain.py\ntest_containers.py\ntest_control.py\ntest_emoji.py\ntest_file_proxy.py\ntest_filesize.py\ntest_getfileno.py\ntest_highlighter.py\ntest_inspect.py\ntest_json.py\ntest_jupyter.py\ntest_layout.py\ntest_live.py\ntest_live_render.py\ntest_log.py\ntest_logging.py\ntest_markdown.py\ntest_markdown_no_hyperlinks.py\ntest_markup.py\ntest_measure.py\ntest_null_file.py\ntest_padding.py\ntest_palette.py\ntest_panel.py\ntest_pick.py\ntest_pretty.py\ntest_progress.py\ntest_prompt.py\ntest_protocol.py\ntest_ratio.py\ntest_repr.py\ntest_rich_print.py\ntest_rule.py\ntest_rule_in_table.py\ntest_screen.py\ntest_segment.py\ntest_spinner.py\ntest_stack.py\ntest_status.py\ntest_style.py\ntest_styled.py\ntest_syntax.py\ntest_table.py\ntest_text.py\ntest_theme.py\ntest_tools.py\ntest_traceback.py\ntest_tree.py\ntest_unicode_data.py\ntest_win32_console.py\ntest_windows_renderer.py\n"}],"isError":false,"durationMs":22,"timestamp":1794168274221},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I'm checking the top-level `rich/__init__.py` `__all__` at line 8, since that's where the spec's `rich.__all__` points. I'll also look at how a simple component like `rule.py` or `styled.py` is structured, and weigh whether building a `Text` or working with segments directly is the cleaner route.\n\nI'm weighing how to render the badge: either build a `Text` and let it draw itself, or assemble the segments by hand so the border style applies to the caps. Building segments manually looks like the better fit. I'd resolve the style string through `console.get_style`, which handles bot